  "query_preview": "..\nThe forecast remains in good shape. Isolated trade-wind showers are\nto be expected through at least the beginning of next week. This\nshould be considered the first taste of the dry season. So far this \nmonth we have had 0.40 inches of rain, about 2.28 inches lower than\nthe average so far for November. While the Marianas are not yet in\ndrought conditions, drought conditions may be anticipated later this\ndry season. For now, fairer weather days are expected, but try to \nstart conserving water as we are transitioning into the dry season.\n\nMarine...\nLight to moderate easterly trade winds continue this week,\nbecoming northeasterly around Wednesday evening. Combined seas of\n5 to 7 ft build to 6 to 8 feet by late week due to building \nnorthwest to north swell, emanating from distant Typhoon Cempaka.\nSeas and surf are not expected to exceed advisory thresholds at this\ntime.\n\nEastern Micronesia...\nA fairly quiet period is in store for the region. A weakening Near-\nEquatorial Trough (NET) stretches from Chuuk to Pohnpei, diving\nsoutheast as it passes by Kosrae to the northeast, to Makin in the\nsouthern RMI (Republic of the Marshall Islands). Near the trough,\nwinds are light and variable. Winds then become southwest south of\nthe trough, and northeast to the north of the trough. The NET has\nshown a weakening signature in convective activity during the day \n(similar to yesterday), after a nocturnal increase was seen last \nnight into this morning (typical nocturnal flare-up). This may occur \nonce again tonight, but the cumulus field is less agitated today \ncompared to yesterday, and numerical forecasts also show a decreasing\nprecipitation signature. \n\nThus, look for continued dry weather the next several days at \nPohnpei and Kosrae. At Majuro, a weak trough may bring scattered \nshowers to the area Thursday night into Friday. otherwise, a \ncontinued dry theme will prevail, with just \"hit-and- miss\" showers \nthe most we can hope for.\n\nAs for our mariners, light to gentle winds will prevail, with seas of\n4 to 6 feet expected area-wide through the forecast period.",
